---
import BlogLayoutEn from "../../../layouts/BlogLayoutEn.astro";

const faqSchema = [
  {
    question: "When is the PHP2Shell patch released?",
    answer: "PHP scheduled it for Wednesday 21 October 2026 at 14:00 UTC. The PHP team warned it could ship earlier if details went public or there was active exploitation, and both have already happened."
  },
  {
    question: "Is my server vulnerable to PHP2Shell?",
    answer: "If it serves PHP reachable from the internet, most likely yes. According to Sansec and Flare, every version from PHP 5.4 to the current ones is affected, including 8.2.34, 8.3.35, 8.4.26 and 8.5.11. Branches 8.1 and older will not get a patch."
  },
  {
    question: "What should I do if I can't update today?",
    answer: "Apply layered mitigations: block multipart/form-data at the web server, disable session.upload_progress, use a WAF without leaving the origin directly reachable, or take the application offline. None of them replaces the patch."
  }
];
---

<BlogLayoutEn
  title="Does Your Website Run PHP? It Could Be Attacked Today"
  description="PHP2Shell: PHP ships a patch today for two critical code execution flaws already being exploited. Which servers are at risk and what to do in the next hours."
  publishDate="2026-10-21"
  author="AI Security"
  readTime="4 min"
  tags={["PHP", "Vulnerabilities", "CVE", "Alert", "Cybersecurity"]}
  canonical="https://aisecurity.es/en/blog/php2shell-parche-php-hoy-actualizar"
  faqSchema={faqSchema}>

  <div class="my-6 rounded-xl p-5" style="background:rgba(239,68,68,0.12);border:1px solid rgba(239,68,68,0.45);">
    <p style="max-width:none;margin:0;color:#fecaca;">
      <strong style="color:#ffffff;">Critical alert · 21 October 2026.</strong> PHP releases a patch today at <strong style="color:#ffffff;">14:00 UTC</strong> for two remote code execution vulnerabilities in the interpreter itself. Exploitation is already underway. If you run PHP exposed to the internet, your server is at risk <strong style="color:#ffffff;">right now</strong>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    They are called <strong>PHP2Shell</strong>: <strong>CVE-2026-109618</strong> and <strong>CVE-2026-109619</strong>, both rated CVSS v4.0 <strong>9.2 (critical)</strong>. They let an attacker <strong>with no credentials</strong> run code on the server with a single HTTP request, on default PHP installs. Your application doesn't need to have any bug: the problem is in PHP, before your code even runs.
  </p>

  <h2>Why this one is serious</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Details leaked before the patch.</strong> PHP had to bring the notice forward to 20 October and the release to the 21st.</li>
    <li><strong>It is already being exploited.</strong> Cloudflare saw the first attacks at 07:43 UTC on 21 October, and Aikido confirmed real exploitation that same morning. Public proofs of concept are circulating.</li>
    <li><strong>It affects almost everything.</strong> According to Sansec and Flare, every version since PHP 5.4. That includes WordPress, WooCommerce, Magento, Shopware, Laravel, Drupal, PrestaShop and any PHP website or panel.</li>
    <li><strong>Mass attacks arrive within minutes.</strong> Sansec expects automated scanning less than 15 minutes after the patch ships, once the fixed code reveals exactly where the flaw is.</li>
    <li><strong>Organisations are switching services off.</strong> In the Netherlands, Tweakers, the Tweede Kamer and the NCSC took websites offline as a precaution.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Is your server affected?</h2>

  <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full text-sm" style="border-collapse:collapse;">
      <thead>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.15);">
          <th style="padding:10px;text-align:left;color:#c4b5fd;">Your PHP version</th>
          <th style="padding:10px;text-align:left;color:#c4b5fd;">Status</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody style="color:rgba(255,255,255,0.8);">
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);"><td style="padding:10px;font-weight:bold;">8.2 · 8.3 · 8.4 · 8.5</td><td style="padding:10px;">Vulnerable until you install today's fixed release. The latest versions published before the patch (8.2.34, 8.3.35, 8.4.26, 8.5.11) <strong>are still vulnerable</strong>.</td></tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);"><td style="padding:10px;font-weight:bold;">8.1 · 8.0</td><td style="padding:10px;"><strong>Vulnerable with no official patch</strong>. End of life.</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding:10px;font-weight:bold;">7.x and older</td><td style="padding:10px;"><strong>Vulnerable with no official patch</strong>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Being on "a modern version" <strong>does not protect you</strong>. Until today at 14:00 UTC there is no fixed PHP version at all.
  </p>

  <h2>What to do today, in this order</h2>

  <ol>
    <li><strong>Take inventory now.</strong> List every server, hosting account and container running PHP reachable from the internet, including forgotten admin panels and old websites.</li>
    <li><strong>Mitigate before 14:00 UTC.</strong> Don't wait for the patch: block <code>multipart/form-data</code> requests in nginx or Apache, disable <code>session.upload_progress</code> and, if you use Cloudflare, close direct access to your origin server. We explain it step by step, with copy-paste configuration, in <a href="/blog/php2shell-cve-2026-109618-mitigar-sin-parche" hreflang="es">PHP2Shell: how to mitigate it without a patch</a> (in Spanish; the config blocks work as-is).</li>
    <li><strong>Switch off anything non-essential.</strong> A corporate website down for a few hours costs less than a compromised server.</li>
    <li><strong>Update as soon as the patch ships.</strong> Install the new release for your branch (or your distribution's package as soon as it is published) and <strong>restart PHP-FPM or Apache</strong>: without a restart the vulnerable binary keeps running.</li>
    <li><strong>If you are on 8.1 or older</strong>, plan the migration to 8.2+ today and keep the mitigations or isolation in place until it's done.</li>
    <li><strong>Check whether you've already been breached.</strong> Look for new or modified PHP files, web shells, new admin users and unusual outbound connections.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    On shared hosting? Email your provider today and ask which PHP version serves your site and when they will apply the patch. Don't assume they'll do it on their own.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>

  <div class="faq-accordion">
    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>
        <span>When is the PHP2Shell patch released?</span>
        <svg class="faq-chevron" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2.5" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/></svg>
      </summary>
      <div class="faq-answer">
        <p>PHP scheduled it for Wednesday 21 October 2026 at 14:00 UTC. The PHP team warned it could ship earlier if details went public or there was active exploitation, and both have already happened.</p>
      </div>
    </details>
    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>
        <span>Is my server vulnerable to PHP2Shell?</span>
        <svg class="faq-chevron" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2.5" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/></svg>
      </summary>
      <div class="faq-answer">
        <p>If it serves PHP reachable from the internet, most likely yes. According to Sansec and Flare, every version from PHP 5.4 to the current ones is affected, including 8.2.34, 8.3.35, 8.4.26 and 8.5.11. Branches 8.1 and older will not get a patch.</p>
      </div>
    </details>
    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>
        <span>What should I do if I can't update today?</span>
        <svg class="faq-chevron" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2.5" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/></svg>
      </summary>
      <div class="faq-answer">
        <p>Apply layered mitigations: block multipart/form-data at the web server, disable session.upload_progress, use a WAF without leaving the origin directly reachable, or take the application offline. None of them replaces the patch.</p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>

